      "title": "Cue on ISA search engine choice",
      "summary": "Cue acknowledged the ISA didn't allow Apple to offer search engine choice during setup, arguing it would be a bad user experience to show engines users haven't heard of."
    },
    {
      "title": "Cue on Google default selection",
      "summary": "Cue stated Apple makes Google the default search engine because it is the best, allowing users to easily change it, though DuckDuckGo's CEO testified switching takes too many steps."
    },
    {
      "title": "Cue on Apple privacy vs Google",
      "summary": "Cue agreed Apple believes user privacy is important and has better privacy than Google, citing ISA provisions requiring Google to allow searching without logging in and Apple's efforts to prevent tracking."
    },
    {
      "title": "Bellshaw on Google payments",
      "summary": "Bellshaw questioned if the ISA represents a significant portion of Apple's profits; Cue replied Apple does not view it that way because the agreement works due to the platform's appeal."
    },
    {
      "title": "Schmidtlein on Safari and Google history",
      "summary": "Google lawyer John Schmidtlein led Cue through Safari's history, noting the seamless Google integration was a key user interface innovation mentioned in early promotional materials before 2003."
    },
    {
      "title": "Cue on pre-2003 search behavior",
      "summary": "Cue explained that before 2003, users had to type google.com or another URL to search, whereas Apple introduced functionality where typing non-URL text in the URL field goes to search."
    },
    {
      "title": "Schmidtlein on search engine switching",
      "summary": "Schmidtlein argued Google helped Safari succeed by being a great product, referencing deals with Yahoo and Bing, and both men claimed switching search engines is easy and a non-issue."
    },
    {
      "title": "DOJ questions on Google market dominance",
      "summary": "The DOJ questions whether Google is the best search engine or just pays the most, and if payments stopped, what the market would look like; Cue said Apple never thought about this, and Google agreed."
    }
  ]
}

